 ## Overview
-The Level-0 Mask Classifier is a binary classification method used within hierarchical neural volume representations (specifically NeuralVDB) to predict whether a given voxel coordinate corresponds to an active (non-background) or inactive (background) value. It serves as the leaf-level occupancy predictor, replacing explicit bitmask storage for the lowest level of the VDB tree hierarchy. Unlike the Level-1 Mask Classifier which handles ternary decisions for internal nodes, this method strictly distinguishes between presence and absence of data at the voxel resolution, enabling sparse field training by excluding inactive background samples from the loss calculation.
+The Level-0 Mask Classifier is a binary occupancy network that predicts whether a given voxel coordinate corresponds to an active or inactive value within a hierarchical VDB tree [S1]. It replaces explicit bitmask storage at the leaf level, distinguishing itself from sibling methods by operating strictly at voxel resolution to enable sparse field training [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** 3D voxel coordinates $(x, y, z)$ normalized to the grid space.
-*   **Output:** A binary prediction indicating if the voxel is active ($1$) or inactive ($0$).
